## Agent Behavior Rules

### ALWAYS Use Worktrees — CRITICAL
- **Call `EnterWorktree` at the start of EVERY session before editing any files.** Do not wait for the user to tell you about parallel sessions — you will not always know. The cost of an unnecessary worktree is near zero; the cost of a collision (silent overwrites, lost edits) is high.
- The ONLY exception: the user explicitly tells you "you're the only session, skip worktree."
- When spawning sub-agents via the `Agent` tool for work that edits files, always set `isolation: "worktree"` so each agent gets its own copy of the repo.
- The user has authorized worktree usage as project policy — you do not need an explicit per-session request.

### Branch Naming Convention
- **Always use descriptive branch names.** Format: `<type>/<short-description>` (e.g., `feat/user-auth`, `fix/null-metadata`, `refactor/format-utils`).
- Types: `feat/`, `fix/`, `refactor/`, `chore/`, `docs/`.
- If `EnterWorktree` auto-generates a name, rename the branch before committing: `git branch -m <new-name>`.

### Worktree Hygiene
- **Prune stale worktrees at session start.** Run `git worktree list` and compare against `.claude/worktrees/`. Orphaned directories or branches already merged to main are stale — remove them with `git worktree remove <path>`.
- **Never leave a worktree behind after delivering work.** Always clean up via `ExitWorktree` after the PR is merged.
- **One worktree per task.** Don't reuse a worktree from a previous task — create fresh from current HEAD.

### Worktree Enforcement Hooks
- **UserPromptSubmit hook** — warns about parallel sessions, suppresses when already in a worktree (`$PWD` contains `/.claude/worktrees/`).
- **PreToolUse hook (Edit|Write)** — hard-blocks file edits when parallel sessions detected and NOT in a worktree.
- Hooks count all `claude` processes system-wide (includes other projects). This is intentionally conservative — false positive (forced worktree when unnecessary) is safer than false negative (collision).

### ALWAYS Deliver Your Work — CRITICAL
- **Work that isn't merged to main doesn't exist.** Never end a session with code sitting on an unmerged branch.
